WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:05.000
Lesson 9.6 · Financial Management
Financial controls, approvals and procurement

2
00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:11.800
Set up money checks before the first payment: they are far harder to add later.

3
00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:25.911
Financial controls are the checks that prevent errors and misuse and make your records trustworthy. They are far easier to set up before your first payment than to add later.

4
00:00:26.311 --> 00:00:26.911
Four control principles

5
00:00:26.911 --> 00:00:37.870
Approvals: Spending is approved within agreed limits, using your delegation of authority from Module 3. For example: Either founder approves up to US$200; above that, both must agree.

6
00:00:37.870 --> 00:00:48.459
Evidence: Every transaction has a receipt, invoice or contract behind it. For example: The bakery photographs each flour and stall receipt and files it the same day.

7
00:00:48.459 --> 00:00:58.678
Separation of duties: Requesting, approving, paying and checking go to different people wherever possible. For example: One founder pays suppliers; the other checks the bank statement each month.

8
00:00:58.678 --> 00:01:10.837
Independent review: Someone outside day-to-day work checks the numbers, especially when duties cannot be separated. For example: The accountant on the advisory group reviews the bank reconciliations every quarter.

9
00:01:11.237 --> 00:01:11.837
Why controls protect you

10
00:01:11.837 --> 00:01:17.511
1. They protect your money, and the founders, from errors and suspicion.

11
00:01:17.511 --> 00:01:23.185
2. Funders and banks look for them before trusting you with money.

12
00:01:23.185 --> 00:01:29.489
3. Good habits now are much easier than changing them later.

13
00:01:29.889 --> 00:01:30.489
Where they apply

14
00:01:30.489 --> 00:01:36.163
1. Your bank account: separate from day one and reconciled every month.

15
00:01:36.163 --> 00:01:42.207
2. Your procurement thresholds: when you need quotes and how you choose suppliers.

16
00:01:42.207 --> 00:01:48.881
3. Your conflict of interest declarations, for example buying from a relative.

17
00:01:49.281 --> 00:01:49.881
What to avoid

18
00:01:49.881 --> 00:01:54.815
Mixing personal and initiative money in one bank account.

19
00:01:54.815 --> 00:01:59.748
Pretending duties are separate when one person does everything.

20
00:01:59.748 --> 00:02:06.422
Copying another country’s rules as if they were your legal duties.

21
00:02:07.022 --> 00:02:08.822
You map one money flow and write your first procurement thresholds.

22
00:02:08.822 --> 00:02:12.822
Then save it, and download it as a PDF.

23
00:02:14.322 --> 00:02:17.422
Method into Impact
Better foundations. Stronger organisations.
